Blocked: this gh write subcommand does not name the repository it targets.

  command:           gh pr comment 55 --body x
  unnamed target:    gh pr comment
  working directory: {{CWD}}
  origin remote:     {{REMOTE}}

Left as it is, gh resolves the target from the working directory, so the origin
above (or whatever remote gh settles on) is what gets written to. Having cd'd
into another repository to look at something is enough to send the issue or the
pull request there instead.

Fix: name the repository with -R owner/repo and run again.
  gh pr comment -R owner/repo ...
Naming the target by its full URL works too — the URL holds the repository:
  gh pr comment https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123 ...
A number on its own, or a branch name, is resolved against the working
directory's remote and so names nothing.

Even when the current directory's remote is certainly the right one, pass the
value from
  gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner
to -R rather than leaving it implicit.
